+# espFirmware
+
+The steps an ESP-IDF firmware file's stages call. `build` builds the firmware with `idf.py`, and
+`upload` uploads it to the devices over the air, through IoTSupport. The steps carry what is the
+same in every firmware build: the container the build runs in, git's safe-directory setting, the
+`idf.py` command line, the upload target and the IoTSupport client secret. A value that differs per
+repo is an argument with no default: the directory the repo is checked out into and, for a repo
+that builds for several hardware versions, the version.
+
+**A Replay is an OTA deploy.** Every build of a firmware job ends in `upload`, so a Replay or a
+rebuild re-flashes the devices over the air. There is no dry run.
+
+The pod declares the `idf` container as a named `images:` entry ([`podYaml`](podYaml.md)), on the
+`jenkins-agent-large` template. The ESP-IDF version is the file's own, in that image's tag, so a
+version bump is one repo's commit. The steps do not clone: the file's `Checkout` stage checks out
+the repo, `esp-libs` beside it and any other repo the build reads, each into a directory of its
+own. Each step is a call on the var's method, so a declarative stage runs it in `script {}`. The
+file still writes out its every stage; the steps go inside them.
+
+```groovy
+agent {
+    kubernetes {
+        inheritFrom 'jenkins-agent-large'
+        yaml podYaml(images: [[image: 'espressif/idf:v5.5.3', name: 'idf']])
+    }
+}
+
+stages {
+    stage('Checkout') {
+        steps {
+            dir('PaperClock') {
+                checkout scm
+            }
+            dir('esp-libs') {
+                git url: 'https://github.com/pvginkel/esp-libs.git', branch: 'main',
+                    credentialsId: '5f6fbd66-b41c-405f-b107-85ba6fd97f10'
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    stage('Build firmware') {
+        steps {
+            script {
+                espFirmware.build(dir: 'PaperClock')
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    stage('Deploy firmware') {
+        steps {
+            script {
+                espFirmware.upload(dir: 'PaperClock')
+            }
+        }
+    }
+}
+```
+
+## Steps
+
+`dir` is required. A missing argument, an empty one or one the step does not take fails the build,
+and the error names it.
+
+### build
+
+`espFirmware.build(dir: '<dir>')` runs, in the `idf` container and in the directory `dir`,
+`git config --global --add safe.directory '*'` and then `idf.py build`, through the image's
+`/opt/esp/entrypoint.sh`. The ESP-IDF image does not run as the agent's uid, and git refuses a
+checkout another uid owns; the setting lets the build read the repo's git state.
+
+`espFirmware.build(dir: '<dir>', hardwareVersion: <n>)` builds for hardware version `n`:
+`idf.py -DHARDWARE_VERSION=<n> build`. Each version builds into the one `build/` directory, so a
+file that builds several uploads each before it builds the next.
+
+| Argument          | What it is                                                                 |
+| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `dir`             | The directory the file's `Checkout` stage checked the repo out into.       |
+| `hardwareVersion` | Optional: the hardware version to build for, a whole number such as `2`.   |
+
+### upload
+
+`espFirmware.upload(dir: '<dir>')` runs, in the `idf` container and in the directory `dir`, the
+repo's `scripts/upload.sh https://iot.ginbov.nl`, which uploads the firmware the last `build` made
+to IoTSupport for the devices. The step reads the IoTSupport client secret,
+`kv/jenkins/iotsupport-pipeline-oidc`, with `withVault` around the upload alone
+([SEC-1](../guide/secrets.md#sec-1)), into `IOTSUPPORT_CLIENT_ID` and `IOTSUPPORT_CLIENT_SECRET`.
+The file names no secret, and none reaches the pod spec ([SEC-2](../guide/secrets.md#sec-2)).
+
+| Argument | What it is                                                           |
+| -------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `dir`    | The directory the file's `Checkout` stage checked the repo out into. |
+
+## Internals
+
+`buildStep`, `uploadDirectory` and `arguments` check the arguments and work out where the steps run
+and what `build` runs. They are not part of what `espFirmware` offers a Jenkinsfile.

